# Native engines only. Overwriting the SAME key of a one-entry map, from a
# helper called once per loop iteration, grows time and memory without bound.
# A native map is an append-only log of pairs ("any duplicate to be resolved
# on read", runtime.c k_b_put), so every overwrite adds a pair, and the
# helper's evacuation copies the whole log each call.
#
# Should: finish instantly with a map of one entry, as --interp does.
#
# Does (kanso play / kanso build):
#   n = 4000    ~0.5 s
#   n = 8000    ~3 s
#   n = 12000   4.4 GB resident (VmHWM), then finishes
# With a string key instead of 7, n = 4000 takes 41 s.
fn bump m i
  put m 7 i

fn loop m 0
  m

fn loop m n
  loop (bump m n) (n - 1)

print "{(loop {} 8000)[7]}"
